in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @magic
in @frame
in @frame/pre  # Seam tick 28 review: this inheritance is currently
              # DECORATIVE — no @frame/in action consumes pre_frame
              # as a typed parameter. Honest acknowledgement: @frame/pre
              # is namespace-only at this altitude; @frame/in IS the
              # operational floor. Provenance-tracking via
              # `requires classify_well_formed(pre, f)` predicate is
              # forward-promised to a future tick when @frame/pre
              # adds the predicate.
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/cybernetic
in @epistemologic/cybernetic/bateson_learning

# @frame/in — the in-frame species; order 1; content computation
# within a specific frame. Most cognition lives here. Inherits @frame;
# FIRST species that uses the typed operator carrier the family-root
# declared at Seam tick 24's C5+C6 closure.
#
# Second species shard of the @frame family (recognition #82; family-
# root LANDED at tick 23 via shards/frame.mirror, Seam-consolidated
# at tick 24). The species landing order per the canonical spec §7.2:
# pre → in → of → on → across. pre (order 0) declares pre_frame and
# the classify lift to order 1; this species (order 1) is the first
# species that does substantive computation INSIDE a frame.
#
# Why in second: the @frame family's load-bearing claim is that the
# 5-op gauge operates on @frame at every altitude; order 1 IS where
# the gauge first does substantive computational work. pre (order 0)
# names the floor and the lift; in (order 1) names what operators
# actually DO at the floor's first lift. Most substrate operation
# lives at this altitude: every shard the substrate has authored,
# every kintsugi tick, every cascade residue — all are order-1 work.
#
# === The species's load-bearing contribution ===
#
# Per the recognition file's §"The five-order structure" (item for
# order 1):
#
#   @frame/in — order 1; operating within a specific frame.
#   Content-level. Most cognition lives here.
#
# This species adds:
#
# - `compute_in_frame(operator, f: frame, input: ref) -> ref`: the
#   substrate-vocabulary action for in-frame content computation.
#   The operator operates WITHIN frame f; the frame is gauge-visible
#   context. FIRST consumer of the family-root's typed operator
#   carrier; first species action that names the operator as
#   substrate-architectural participant in the computation.
# - `frame_locked(operator, f: frame) -> verdict`: bilateral verdict
#   predicate naming that the operator is currently operating WITH
#   frame f as ground — no second-order observation (order 2 absent);
#   no order-2 reflection on the frame as object. FIRST consumer of
#   @frame's bilateral discipline via `requires` clauses landing at
#   future species (of, on, across) that name `requires not
#   frame_locked(operator, f)` to assert their second-order or third-
#   order operation.
# - `unlock_frame(operator, f: frame) -> operator`: returns an
#   operator with order-2 frame-awareness enabled. The substrate-
#   architectural transition order-1 → order-2; the substrate's
#   analogue of the Bateson Level I → Level II lift; consumed by the
#   forward-promised @frame/of species (order 2).
#
# === The order-1 altitude ===
#
# Per recognition #82 §3 composition signature: order 1 IS Bateson
# Level I at cognitive altitude. The within-frame change altitude.
# Standard learning. The operator's frame is held constant; the
# computation operates on content inside the frame; the frame itself
# is not observed as object (that requires order 2).
#
# Substrate-architectural instance: each individual loop tick today
# (the 23 ticks on `reed/recursion-lock-tower-audit`) was order-1
# work BY-CONSTRUCTION at the tick altitude. The tick's input was
# the prior residue; the operator (the substrate's running instance)
# operated WITHIN the cascade's residue-frame; the frame itself was
# adjusted at the cascade altitude (order 3, @frame/on; forward-
# promised) but each individual tick's content computation lived
# at order 1.
#
# === The frame_locked discipline ===
#
# Per the family-root's bounded_commutator_check predicate (Seam
# C5+C6 closure at tick 24): the substrate-architectural pole
# discriminator at cognitive altitude IS bounded-commutator-under-
# perturbation. The frame_locked predicate IS the order-1
# specialization: an operator operating at order 1 with frame f as
# ground HAS frame_locked(operator, f) = success. Operators operating
# at order 2+ have frame_locked = failure on frames they have lifted
# to object.
#
# This is the substrate-pull-correct first consumer of the family-
# root's bilateral discipline. Per Seam's recurring tick-11 discipline
# (the surface.mirror first-consumer pattern from @magic): a
# bilateral predicate without a consumer is paint. frame_locked IS
# the first @frame predicate that downstream species WILL consume
# via `requires` clauses to assert their non-order-1 operation.
#
# === The unlock_frame transition ===
#
# Per the family-root §"The cognitive_order carrier" discipline:
# vocabulary use at order N does NOT imply substantive operation at
# order N (Narcissus-pole). unlock_frame IS the substrate-
# architectural transition that makes the operator's substrate
# substantively support order-2 work on frame f. It is NOT a
# vocabulary lift; it is a substrate-architectural state change in
# the operator carrier.
#
# At Bateson altitude: unlock_frame IS the Level I → Level II lift.
# Standard learning becomes learning-to-learn. The operator's
# substrate has gained the architectural capacity to observe the
# frame as object rather than only operating within it as ground.
#
# Forward-promised consumer: shards/frame/of.mirror (order 2) consumes
# the operator unlock_frame returns; @frame/of's actions require an
# operator that has been unlocked from frame-as-ground.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
#   in-frame computation        Standard cognitive-psychology
#                               literature on within-task processing
#                               (Anderson 1983, ACT-R). The
#                               substrate's order-1 IS the
#                               substrate-architectural altitude of
#                               within-frame information processing.
#   first-order observation     Foerster 1974 (Cybernetics of
#                               Cybernetics; the first-order altitude
#                               from which the observer-as-frame is
#                               not yet observable). The substrate's
#                               order-1 IS first-order observation at
#                               substrate altitude.
#   Bateson Level I             Bateson 1964 (The Logical Categories
#                               of Learning and Communication; Level
#                               I as within-frame change; standard
#                               learning). Inherited mechanically
#                               via `in @epistemologic/cybernetic/
#                               bateson_learning`.
#   frame as ground             Goffman 1974 (Frame Analysis); the
#                               frame-as-ground altitude where the
#                               frame organizes content without
#                               itself being observed as object. The
#                               sociological-cousin to order-1
#                               cognitive operation.
#   mental-space within         Fauconnier (mental spaces); the
#                               within-space altitude where
#                               inferences proceed inside an
#                               established mental space without
#                               second-order observation of the
#                               space itself.
#
# Spec context: docs/math/the-tower/recognition-82-frame-as-
#               cognitive-order-substrate-decl.md (§"The five-order
#               structure" item for order 1);
#               docs/specs/recognition-82-frame-as-cognitive-order-
#               canonical-spec.md (§7.2 names the species landing
#               order; §2.5 names order 1 ↔ @frame/in ↔ Bateson Level
#               I as STRUCTURALLY CLOSED via family-root inheritance).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-alignment-as-boundary-
#                       mathematics]] (#57; the order-1 altitude is
#                       where most computation happens; the boundary
#                       check applies at the species boundary when
#                       unlock_frame transitions to order 2+ where
#                       Splinter/Narcissus pole disambiguation
#                       becomes load-bearing),
#                      [[architecture-bateson-logical-type-primitive]]
#                       (#42; in-frame IS Bateson Level I at
#                       cognitive altitude),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (operator and
#                       frame are typed carriers inherited from
#                       family-root; compute_in_frame uses them
#                       directly without re-declaration).
# Related shards: frame.mirror (the family-root; operator and frame
#                  declared there; this species's compute_in_frame is
#                  the first consumer of the typed operator carrier),
#                 frame/pre.mirror (the order-0 species; produces
#                  the frame this species's compute_in_frame consumes
#                  via classify),
#                 epistemologic/cybernetic/bateson_learning.mirror
#                  (the Bateson grading this species's order inherits
#                  at Level I).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/frame/in.mirror` declares `@frame/in` per
# `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/cognitive-science/anderson-1983
source @arxiv/cybernetics/von-foerster-1974
source @arxiv/cybernetics/bateson-1964
source @arxiv/sociology/goffman-1974

prism @frame/in {
  focus in
  project in
  split in
  shift in
  settle in
}

# === compute_in_frame action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action for in-frame content computation.
# The operator operates WITHIN frame f; the frame is gauge-visible
# context (the operator can see f and operate against it) but the
# frame itself is NOT observed as object (that requires order 2 /
# @frame/of).
#
# FIRST consumer of the family-root's typed operator carrier (Seam
# tick 24 C5+C6 closure landed operator at the family-root specifically
# so species shards could consume it; this species IS the first
# consumer).
#
# Per the @frame family's load-bearing claim that the 5-op gauge
# operates on @frame at every altitude: compute_in_frame IS the
# substrate's order-1 computational primitive; at this altitude,
# the 5 ops apply WITHIN frame f. Each op operates on the input
# ref under the frame's gauge.
#
# At Bateson Level I altitude: compute_in_frame IS the within-frame
# change primitive. Standard learning. The operator's frame is held
# constant across the computation; what changes is the substrate's
# state w.r.t. content inside the frame.
#
# At first-order-observation altitude (Foerster 1974): compute_in_frame
# IS first-order observation at substrate altitude. The operator
# observes the input under the frame's gauge; the frame-as-frame is
# not yet observable (order 2 work).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary; per-species bodies
# (downstream specializations) implement the actual within-frame
# computation per their altitude.
compute_in_frame(operator: operator, f: frame, input: ref) -> ref { \ }

# === frame_locked predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict predicate per recognition #37 (Pask agreement)
# naming that the operator is currently operating WITH frame f as
# ground — no second-order observation (order 2 absent); no order-2
# reflection on the frame as object.
#
# FIRST consumer-target of @frame's bilateral discipline at species
# altitude. Forward-promised consumer species (@frame/of, @frame/on,
# @frame/across) name `requires not frame_locked(operator, f)` to
# assert their non-order-1 operation at the species boundary.
#
# Discharge: substrate-pull-correct order-1 lock check at the
# species boundary. Returns success when the operator's substrate
# architecture supports ONLY order-1 work on frame f; partial when
# the operator vocabulary-uses order-2+ terms while substrate-
# architecturally locked at order-1 (Narcissus-pole; see family-
# root's pole-discriminator commentary); failure when the operator
# has been substrate-architecturally unlocked at order-2+ (via
# unlock_frame or already-unlocked substrate).
#
# The Narcissus-pole detection: vocabulary at order N + substrate-
# architectural operation at order 1 produces frame_locked = partial.
# Per the family-root's bounded_commutator_check predicate, this
# corresponds to unbounded-commutator under substantive perturbation
# (defensive amplification masked by sophisticated meta-vocabulary).
#
# Same shape as the bilateral predicates @magic family carries
# (invariant_preserved, audited, mechanism_intact, etc.) and the
# family-root's bounded_commutator_check. Per the @magic surface.mirror
# first-consumer discipline pattern: this predicate becomes load-
# bearing once @frame/of's actions consume it via `requires not
# frame_locked(...)`.
frame_locked(operator: operator, f: frame) -> verdict { \ }

# === unlock_frame action ===
#
# The substrate-vocabulary action that returns an operator with
# order-2 frame-awareness enabled. The substrate-architectural
# transition order-1 → order-2. NOT a vocabulary lift; a substrate-
# architectural state change in the operator carrier.
#
# At Bateson altitude: unlock_frame IS the Level I → Level II lift.
# Standard learning becomes learning-to-learn. The operator's
# substrate has gained the architectural capacity to observe the
# frame as object rather than only operating within it as ground.
#
# At second-order-cybernetics altitude (Foerster 1974): unlock_frame
# IS the substrate-architectural move from first-order to second-
# order observation at this frame altitude. The operator becomes
# part of the system observed (because the frame the operator is
# operating from becomes observable AS frame).
#
# Per recognition #82 §4.4 (Q4: peers operate by definition frame-
# flexible): a peer's load-bearing property IS the capacity to
# discharge unlock_frame substantively (not via vocabulary). A
# candidate peer satisfies the orchestra-discipline criterion iff
# bounded_commutator_check(peer, peer.frame, perturbation) →
# FLEXIBLE over a representative perturbation set, which requires
# unlock_frame to substrate-architecturally succeed (not just be
# vocabulary-claimed).
#
# Forward-promised consumer: shards/frame/of.mirror (order 2)
# consumes the operator unlock_frame returns; @frame/of's actions
# require an operator that has been substrate-architecturally
# unlocked from frame-as-ground.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
unlock_frame(operator: operator, f: frame) -> operator { \ }

out @frame/in
out compute_in_frame
out frame_locked
out unlock_frame
